  {
    "id": "pescience-select-protein",
    "name": "PEScience Select Protein",
    "brand": "PEScience",
    "badge": "Best Powder for DIY Pancakes",
    "category": "Whey and casein blend protein powder",
    "description": "If you would rather build your own batter, PEScience Select is our pick for the protein powder. It is built on milk protein isolate, which brings casein alongside whey, and that casein is what keeps a protein pancake moist instead of rubbery. Per the label at the time of writing, a scoop gives about 23 to 24 g of protein in roughly 120 calories. One scoop with an egg, oats and milk makes a stack with more protein than any box.",
    "pros": [
      "Casein content keeps homemade pancakes softer than pure whey isolate",
      "About 23 to 24 g of protein per scoop per the label",
      "Vanilla and chocolate flavors double as pancake flavoring"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium",
      "Costs more per scoop than budget whey concentrates",
      "You still need flour, oats or banana for structure"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The powder to buy if you want protein pancakes with 30 g or more per plate and full control of the ingredients.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 23-24 g per scoop (label)",
      "Calories": "About 120",
      "Protein source": "Milk protein isolate and whey",
      "Best for": "Homemade high-protein batter"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Protein Pancake Mix",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-protein-pancake-mix/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best protein pancake mix?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, Kodiak Power Cakes Buttermilk is the best protein pancake mix: about 14 to 15 g of protein per half-cup serving with just water per the box, a whole-grain flour base and consistently good buyer reports on texture. FlapJacked has more protein per serving, about 20 g, and Krusteaz Protein is the budget pick."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein is in Kodiak pancakes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Per the box at the time of writing, Kodiak Power Cakes Buttermilk gives about 14 to 15 g of protein per half-cup serving made with water, about 18 g made with milk and about 21 g with milk and an egg. Flavored versions such as Dark Chocolate and Pumpkin run a gram or two lower, so check the panel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I just add protein powder to pancake mix?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Stir up to half a scoop into one serving of mix and add an extra tablespoon or two of liquid, because the powder absorbs water. More than that makes the pancakes tough and dry. Casein or whey-casein blends work best; pure whey isolate dries out faster. Cook a little lower and slower than usual."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are protein pancakes good for weight loss?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They can help because protein keeps you fuller than a regular stack, but calories still count. One serving is modest, two servings with milk, egg and syrup add up quickly. Weigh the dry mix, choose protein-rich toppings like Greek yogurt over syrup, and fit the meal into your daily calorie and protein targets."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Kodiak or Birch Benders better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Kodiak is the better all-rounder because of its whole-grain base and consistent texture. Birch Benders Protein is a little easier because it is a complete just-add-water mix with about 15 to 16 g of protein per serving per retailer labels, and buyers describe it as lighter. Choose Kodiak for nutrition, Birch Benders for convenience."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do protein pancake mixes go bad?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They do not spoil quickly, but they do go stale. The fats and dairy proteins in protein mixes lose flavor and rise over time, and old mix makes flat pancakes. Check the best-by date when a box arrives, store it sealed in a cool, dry place, and finish an opened bag within a few months."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are there gluten-free protein pancake mixes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, but they are fewer and usually lower in protein. FlapJacked sells a separately labeled gluten-free mix, and Kodiak has offered gluten-free Power Cakes. Confirm gluten-free in the title and on the box image, read the allergen statement, and if you have celiac disease, ask your doctor about cross-contact risk."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Specialty Protein",
    tags: [
      "protein pancake mix",
      "best protein pancake mix",
      "kodiak protein pancakes",
      "high protein pancake mix",
      "protein powder pancakes"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Updated November 2026"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Pancake Mix</Fragment>
      Seven mixes and one DIY powder ranked on protein per serving as you would actually make them, ingredients, texture reports and cost, plus how to stop protein pancakes turning rubbery.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#quick-comparison">Quick Comparison</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#how-much-protein">How Much Protein, Really?</a>
      <a href="#diy-protein-pancakes">Make Your Own From Powder</a>
      <a href="#are-they-healthy">Are They Healthy?</a>
      <a href="#rubbery-pancakes">Fixing Dry, Rubbery Pancakes</a>
      <a href="#special-diets">Gluten-Free and Dairy-Free</a>
      <a href="#buying-on-amazon">Buying on Amazon</a>
      <a href="#how-we-picked">How We Picked</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best protein pancake mix for most people is <strong>Kodiak Power Cakes Buttermilk</strong>: whole-grain flour, about 14 to 15 g of protein per half-cup serving with just water, and the most consistent buyer reports on texture. If you want the most protein straight from the box, FlapJacked lists about 20 g per serving, and if you already own a good whey-casein powder, making your own batter beats every box on protein per dollar.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Quick Comparison: The Best Protein Pancake Mixes
          </h2>

          <p>
            The short version: Kodiak Power Cakes Buttermilk is the best all-round protein pancake mix, FlapJacked has the most protein per serving from a box, Birch Benders is the easiest just-add-water option, and Krusteaz Protein is the cheapest familiar pancake. If you care most about protein per dollar, skip the box and make your own batter from a casein-rich protein powder such as PEScience Select. Every figure below is per the label at the time of writing and is made with water unless stated, because that is the honest baseline: the big numbers on the front of most boxes assume you add milk and an egg.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Prep for that number</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kodiak Power Cakes Buttermilk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 14-15 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Everyday all-rounder</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">FlapJacked Buttermilk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most protein from a box</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Birch Benders Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15-16 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easiest weekday batter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Premier Protein Complete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15 g (21 g with milk and egg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk-and-egg batter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Krusteaz Protein Buttermilk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 g (about 20 g with milk and egg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget pick</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kodiak Power Cakes Dark Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 14 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flavored mix, kids</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PEScience Select (DIY)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 23-24 g per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Your own batter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest protein per plate</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Servings differ between boxes, from a third of a cup to a half cup of dry mix, so compare protein per serving alongside the serving weight in grams on the panel. A half-cup serving usually makes three or four 4-inch pancakes, which is less than most adults eat in one sitting. Plan on one and a half to two servings per plate, and the protein, calories and sodium all scale with it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Best Protein Pancake Mixes, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These seven are ranked on protein per serving made the way the box tells you, the quality of the flour and protein in the ingredient list, sugar and fiber, cost per box and consistent buyer reports on texture. We did not run a kitchen panel; texture and taste notes summarize what buyers report, and our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page explains the method. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor and box version.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="how-much-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Protein Is Really in a Protein Pancake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Less than the front of the box suggests, unless you follow the box's full recipe. Most protein pancake mixes print two or three protein figures: one for water only, one for milk, and one for milk plus an egg. Krusteaz, for example, lists 15 g with water, about 17 g with milk and about 20 g with milk and an egg, and Premier and Kodiak follow the same pattern. The marketing tends to lead with the biggest number. The grams the mix itself contributes are the water-only figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters because the extra protein from milk and egg is not unique to protein mix. An egg adds about 6 g of protein and a cup of milk about 8 g to any pancake batter, including an ordinary buttermilk mix. A standard pancake mix made with water typically gives somewhere around 4 to 6 g of protein per serving. So the real advantage of a protein mix is roughly 9 to 11 g per serving over a regular mix, which is meaningful but closer to one-third of a scoop of whey than a full shake.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
